{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications","id":1213031,"title":"Investigation rules, when to add complications?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-04-29T06:19:31-07:00","id":1213031,"title":"Investigation rules, when to add complications?","content":"COFD book is a bit unclear it says that as long as you have the proper number of clues the investigation succeeds but that for each clue short of the required total the ST adds a complication.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-02T18:48:19-07:00","id":1214388,"title":"","content":"I think something got broken in the editing, or in the revision from GMC to CofD.<br /> <br /> See page 79: &quot;Once characters reach the required number of Clues, they're able to Uncover the Truth if that's their goal. <b>They can do so without any additional rolls if they spend a number of Clue elements (divided however they like) equal to the required Clues for the investigation.</b> See below for more on Uncovering the Truth.&quot;<br /> <br /> Later, in the section on Uncovering the Truth, no mention is made of Clue Elements. It mentions that the group doesn't have to roll as long as they have the required number of Clues, but not Clue Elements. And as you point out, there's no clear sense of why any group would finish an investigation without the required number of Clues, since it appears to be up to them when the investigation is over.<br /> <br /> This mystery is a shame, because I rather like the gist of the Investigation system, but I can't use it without interpretation and calling on the help of local ghosts.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T00:53:20-07:00","id":1214462,"title":"","content":"Wait, I think I get it, now...? Wow, this is poorly explained.<br /> <br /> Let's say your mystery has a scope of 3, meaning, three clues are required to solve the case. We know that:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Once characters reach the required number of Clues, they\u2019re able to Uncover the Truth if that\u2019s their goal. (79) </div> </div> </div>Let's say the players begin their investigation, and during their first proper sleuthing scene, they fail their first Uncover the Clue roll. As a result:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The character finds a Clue, but it gets the Incomplete tag. (79) </div> </div> </div>Uh oh. Well, why are Clues tagged &quot;Incomplete&quot; bad? Because, according to this unfortunately-worded passage:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Incomplete Clues are useful, but not as much as others. Their Elements may only be used to grant dice to other rolls to uncover other Clues. They may not add to other rolls <b>or be used as part of an effort to solve the final investigation.</b> (80) </div> </div> </div>Ah, so they are Clues, but unlike the Clues you get from succeeding at Uncover the Clue rolls, <i>they don't help you when you try to close the case</i>. What does it mean that you can't use these Incomplete Clues in your effort to close the case? We read:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Once characters have the requisite Clues, they may Uncover the Truth. So long as they have the required number of Clues, this doesn\u2019t require a roll; they have pieced together the puzzle. However, <b>for every Clue short of the required total</b>, the Storyteller adds one significant complication to the investigation. (81) </div> </div> </div>What the rules seem to be saying is that once the players collect their third Clue (recall, this is a 3-scope mystery), no matter what type it is--Tainted, Incomplete, or untagged--the mystery is solvable; players simply have to announce that they wish to solve the mystery, and it is solved. They know the truth, no rolls required. We know that all types of Clues count toward the total required to solve an Investigation, because the rules <i>twice</i> state that players can solve the case <b>once they</b> <b>reach the required number of Clues</b>, and neither instance mentions tags. A Clue is a Clue.<br /> <br /> However, what does it mean that every Clue bearing an Incomplete or Tainted tag is <b>&quot;short of the required total&quot;</b> when Uncovering the Truth? What total? Well, a total equaling the scope of the Investigation: 3. So three Clues of any type can trigger Uncover the Truth, but only three <i>untagged</i> Clues gives you a clean, uncomplicated ending. Tagged Clues add a Complication on a one for one basis.<br /> <br /> This is probably all obvious to some, but I found the writing so unclear that I formed false impressions of how to run these rules. A few false impressions, actually.<br /> <br /> If a group that wants to avoid final Complications gets an Incomplete Clue, they would have to seek out an extra, untagged Clue, lengthening the game to bring their total Clues to 4 (3 to Uncover the Truth + 1 to make up for the Incomplete Clue). A group that wanted a messy ending or shorter game would Uncover the Truth once they got the 3rd Clue, even if all of them were Incomplete or Tainted. They'd just be hit with three Complications, leaving them wiser for knowing the truth but upset that the villain escaped the full brunt of justice.<br /> <br /> The system seems to allow characters to search for as long as they want, seeking additional Clues to make up for tagged ones, avoiding Complications. On the one hand, this lets players decide how and when they want to crack the case, letting them extend their search if they don't like the quality of their Clues. On the other, it may decrease the tension created when players don't know if they can solve the case &quot;in time&quot;. As ST, I'd recommend setting a &quot;clock&quot; that adds 1 Consequence for every Uncover Clue roll the characters make beyond the Scope of the mystery, punishing the characters for moving too slowly. That way, in a 3-Scope Investigation, if players get a 4th and 5th Clue to compensate for two Incomplete Clues they got earlier, they'd crack the case cleanly (No Complications), but they'd trigger two Consequences along the way. For instance, the villains could make two &quot;attacks&quot; on NPCs or locations, gain two &quot;advantages&quot; they didn't have before, or strike twice at a character or characters' Merits or loved ones. I think this might make players feel the heat for dragging their heels.<br /> <br /> And I still don't understand what this means:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They can do so without any additional rolls if they spend a number of Clue elements (divided however they like) equal to the required Clues for the investigation. (79) </div> </div> </div>That part is absent from the rules given later, which seem to be saying that a roll is never needed when Uncovering the Truth--nothing about how Elements come into play. Probably a typo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T08:41:18-07:00","id":1214623,"title":"","content":"asked this because I was reading<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072110-hunter-the-vigil-supplement-for-princesses\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072110-hunter-the-vigil-supplement-for-princesses\"> Hunter the Vigil supplement for Princesses - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072110-hunter-the-vigil-supplement-for-princesses\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072110-hunter-the-vigil-supplement-for-princesses </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Hello, people! I am currently trying to make a fan-made supplement for Hunter the Vigil similar to Nightstalkers, Spirit-Slayers, Witch-hunters, Mortal Remains and </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> and noting when precisely a complication would happen is REALLY relevant to it.<br /> <br /> does it happen only at the final roll or well before your near it and still collecting info?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T10:21:23-07:00","id":1214666,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214462#post1214462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait, I think I get it, now...? Wow, this is poorly explained.<br /> <br /> Let's say your mystery has a scope of 3, meaning, three clues are required to solve the case. We know that:<br /> <br /> Let's say the players begin their investigation, and during their first proper sleuthing scene, they fail their first Uncover the Clue roll. As a result:<br /> <br /> Uh oh. Well, why are Clues tagged &amp;quot;Incomplete&amp;quot; bad? Because, according to this unfortunately-worded passage:<br /> <br /> Ah, so they are Clues, but unlike the Clues you get from succeeding at Uncover the Clue rolls, <i>they don't help you when you try to close the case</i>. What does it mean that you can't use these Incomplete Clues in your effort to close the case? We read:<br /> <br /> What the rules seem to be saying is that once the players collect their third Clue (recall, this is a 3-scope mystery), no matter what type it is--Tainted, Incomplete, or untagged--the mystery is solvable; players simply have to announce that they wish to solve the mystery, and it is solved. They know the truth, no rolls required. We know that all types of Clues count toward the total required to solve an Investigation, because the rules <i>twice</i> state that players can solve the case <b>once they</b> <b>reach the required number of Clues</b>, and neither instance mentions tags. A Clue is a Clue.<br /> <br /> However, what does it mean that every Clue bearing an Incomplete or Tainted tag is <b>&amp;quot;short of the required total&amp;quot;</b> when Uncovering the Truth? What total? Well, a total equaling the scope of the Investigation: 3. So three Clues of any type can trigger Uncover the Truth, but only three <i>untagged</i> Clues gives you a clean, uncomplicated ending. Tagged Clues add a Complication on a one for one basis.<br /> <br /> This is probably all obvious to some, but I found the writing so unclear that I formed false impressions of how to run these rules. A few false impressions, actually.<br /> <br /> If a group that wants to avoid final Complications gets an Incomplete Clue, they would have to seek out an extra, untagged Clue, lengthening the game to bring their total Clues to 4 (3 to Uncover the Truth + 1 to make up for the Incomplete Clue). A group that wanted a messy ending or shorter game would Uncover the Truth once they got the 3rd Clue, even if all of them were Incomplete or Tainted. They'd just be hit with three Complications, leaving them wiser for knowing the truth but upset that the villain escaped the full brunt of justice.<br /> <br /> The system seems to allow characters to search for as long as they want, seeking additional Clues to make up for tagged ones, avoiding Complications. On the one hand, this lets players decide how and when they want to crack the case, letting them extend their search if they don't like the quality of their Clues. On the other, it may decrease the tension created when players don't know if they can solve the case &amp;quot;in time&amp;quot;. As ST, I'd recommend setting a &amp;quot;clock&amp;quot; that adds 1 Consequence for every Uncover Clue roll the characters make beyond the Scope of the mystery, punishing the characters for moving too slowly. That way, in a 3-Scope Investigation, if players get a 4th and 5th Clue to compensate for two Incomplete Clues they got earlier, they'd crack the case cleanly (No Complications), but they'd trigger two Consequences along the way. For instance, the villains could make two &amp;quot;attacks&amp;quot; on NPCs or locations, gain two &amp;quot;advantages&amp;quot; they didn't have before, or strike twice at a character or characters' Merits or loved ones. I think this might make players feel the heat for dragging their heels.</div> </div> </div> </div>You seem to be on the right track, but I think there\u2019s an important drletail you\u2019re missing:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Clues, in the scope of these rules, are a specialized type of equipment (see p. 100)<br /> ...<br /> These resources, called elements, can be used for anything pertaining to that Clue or the investigation at large.<br /> ...<br /> <br /> Elements can be spent for +1 to a relevant dice pool, or as part of the effort to solve the investigation. </div> </div> </div>So, in order to use a Clue to gain an equipment bonus to a relevant roll, you have to <i>spend</i> one of its elements. Meaning unless it has multiple elements (which remember, can be achieved either through exceptional success and/or if the character who uncovers it has 4 or higher in the relevant skill), then using it to gain an equipment bonus will also mean you don\u2019t have enough Elements to solve the mystery without complications unless you go find more Clues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214462#post1214462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I still don't understand what this means:<br /> <br /> That part is absent from the rules given later, which seem to be saying that a roll is never needed when Uncovering the Truth--nothing about how Elements come into play. Probably a typo.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this is most likely an artifact of the draft process that slipped through. However, I think what it\u2019s trying to say is, once you have enough Clues to uncover the truth, with enough elements between them to do so without complications, you don\u2019t need to roll to search for any more Clues.<br /> <br /> The whole section is really messily written. The system itself is ultimately fairly simple. You make rolls to find Clues, each Clue has a number of Elements and may or may not have a Tag, depending on the skill of the investigatior and the results of the roll. You can spend an Element to gain a bonus to a related rolls (potentially modified by any Tags the Clue has), and when you have enough Clues you can uncover the truth, and if you have fewer Elements than the required number of Clues, there are complications. But it took me several careful read-throughs and a bit of educated inference to sort that out, which is a real problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T10:39:21-07:00","id":1214672,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the clarification. However, what exactly is this roll that occurs if you don't have the necessary number of viable Clues? What if you fail? The uncover the truth section does not say, which suggests to me that the game designers changed their mind about a possible roll but forgot to remove an artifact referring to clue elements and possible roll earlier. Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T10:44:41-07:00","id":1214674,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214623#post1214623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">does it happen only at the final roll or well before your near it and still collecting info?</div> </div> </div> </div>It happens when the characters decide to solve the case at the very end. I am inducing this answer from the fact that all of the complications are related to what would happen when investigators try to close the case, not to things that would happen during the investigation itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T12:14:55-07:00","id":1214720,"title":"","content":"so a kindred, princess, beast, slasher doesnt have a chance to learn their being investigated until towards the end?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":1153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","user":"Prince of the Night","date":". 05-03-2018, 03:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T13:42:54-07:00","id":1214749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214672#post1214672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the clarification. However, what exactly is this roll that occurs if you don't have the necessary number of viable Clues?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you don\u2019t have enough Clues to uncover the truth, then naturally the roll you would need to make before you can uncover the truth would be a roll to find another Clue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214672#post1214672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What if you fail?</div> </div> </div> </div>You find a Clue with the Incomplete or Tainted Tag.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214672#post1214672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The uncover the truth section does not say, which suggests to me that the game designers changed their mind about a possible roll but forgot to remove an artifact referring to clue elements and possible roll earlier. Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, it\u2019s very poorly worded and clearly an issue of things changing between drafts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T15:29:57-07:00","id":1214790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214720#post1214720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">so a kindred, princess, beast, slasher doesnt have a chance to learn their being investigated until towards the end?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure what you mean. The Complications, as I understand, aren't a way for the Villain to know they're being investigated. Rather, they are revelations to the characters that although they've learned the truth (IE, who the villain is and what they did), some factor makes bringing them to justice or defeating them difficult or impossible. Here are the Complications:<br /> <br /> \u2022 The culprit has plausible deniability.<br /> \u2022 The culprit has a viable escape plan.<br /> \u2022 The culprit has a hostage.<br /> \u2022 The characters risk legal consequences if they pursue the culprit.<br /> \u2022 The characters risk professional or personal consequences if they pursue the culprit.<br /> \u2022 The crime in question was a smokescreen for a greater conspiracy.<br /> <br /> If the culprit <i>has a hostage</i>, then he/she <i>did</i> know they were being investigated, or they expected someone might try to pursue them. If they have plausible deniability, they either knew they were being investigated or they expected such a possibility and planned ahead. Complications don't tell you <i>when</i> the villain discovered they were being investigated. They tell you that the villain planned things well enough that even though the truth is discovered, they have a way out, or the truth is worse than what the characters expected.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2317,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","user":"Callishka","date":". 05-03-2018, 06:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T15:35:23-07:00","id":1214794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214749#post1214749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you don\u2019t have enough Clues to uncover the truth, then naturally the roll you would need to make before you can uncover the truth would be a roll to find another Clue.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I didn't make myself clear. I understand that once you have the requisite Clues (3 for a 3-scope mystery, 5 for a 5-scope), you don't need to roll anything more. What I'm unclear about is why even mention that you don't need to make an additional roll if you have enough Clue Elements? That makes it sound as if you could have 3 Clues in a 3-scope mystery, but only 2 Clue Elements, and thus need to make a roll. The rules don't say that the potential &quot;additional&quot; roll is for <i>acquiring another clue. </i>They sound more like they're saying there is an Uncover the Truth roll, one that it specific to that stage of the investigation. If they meant that you'd have to go get another Clue, they would say, &quot;without the required number of Clue Elements, the characters must go looking for another Clue&quot;.<br /> <br /> I suspect that there <i>was</i>, at one point, a special roll reserved for the Uncover the Truth stage of the investigation that you could forego if you'd saved enough Elements, but that this roll was nixed, leaving only an indirect reference to it.<br /> <br /> In any case, we agree that this section was poorly written. I'm frankly amazed it got into the final draft, especially after they got a chance to revise it in the transition from GMC to CofD.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2317,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","user":"Callishka","date":". 05-03-2018, 06:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T16:04:27-07:00","id":1214815,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214794#post1214794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I didn't make myself clear. I understand that once you have the requisite Clues (3 for a 3-scope mystery, 5 for a 5-scope), you don't need to roll anything more. What I'm unclear about is why even mention that you don't need to make an additional roll if you have enough Clue Elements? That makes it sound as if you could have 3 Clues in a 3-scope mystery, but only 2 Clue Elements, and thus need to make a roll. The rules don't say that the potential &quot;additional&quot; roll is for <i>acquiring another clue. </i>They sound more like they're saying there is an Uncover the Truth roll, one that it specific to that stage of the investigation. If they meant that you'd have to go get another Clue, they would say, &quot;without the required number of Clue Elements, the characters must go looking for another Clue&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be clear, I agree with you about all of this. If the intent is for the \u201cadditional rolls\u201d referenced to mean rolls to find clues, then it very unclear.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214794#post1214794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I suspect that there <i>was</i>, at one point, a special roll reserved for the Uncover the Truth stage of the investigation that you could forego if you'd saved enough Elements, but that this roll was nixed, leaving only an indirect reference to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>It\u2019s possible. I\u2019m just offering an alternative explanation. Either way, what ended up in the book is very unclear.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Callishka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214794#post1214794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In any case, we agree that this section was poorly written. I'm frankly amazed it got into the final draft, especially after they got a chance to revise it in the transition from GMC to CofD.</div> </div> </div> </div>The investigation rules weren\u2019t in GMC. They, the chase rules, and the Horror creation system were all brand new to CofD and appeared for the first time in that book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-03T19:02:16-07:00","id":1214874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1213031-investigation-rules-when-to-add-complications?p=1214815#post1214815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The investigation rules weren\u2019t in GMC. They, the chase rules, and the Horror creation system were all brand new to CofD and appeared for the first time in that book.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, hmm, that's true. I thought I was remembering my GMC PDF, but it was the CofD PDF I forgot I owned that I first saw the rules in.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2317-callishka","name":"Callishka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2317_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"264"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}